 ## Overview
-Monte Carlo Ambient Occlusion with Distance Mapping is a method to compute ambient occlusion by casting rays in random directions and numerically evaluating the occlusion factor using a distance mapping function [S1, unofficial].
+Monte Carlo Ambient Occlusion with Distance Mapping is a method to compute ambient occlusion by casting rays in random directions and numerically evaluating the occlusion factor using a distance mapping function [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Ambient Occlusion via SDF/Geometry](2527-ambient-occlusion-via-sdf-geometry.md) and differs from [SDF-based Ambient Occlusion](1881-sdf-based-ambient-occlusion.md), [GVDB Ambient Occlusion](2193-gvdb-ambient-occlusion.md), [Screen-Space Ambient Occlusion (SSAO)](3980-screen-space-ambient-occlusion-ssao.md), and [Disk-Based Ambient Occlusion](4333-disk-based-ambient-occlusion.md) by explicitly sampling random directions and evaluating a distance mapping function rather than relying on grid lookups, screen-space projections, or fixed disk patterns [S1, unofficial].
 
